00:01:16.969 --> 00:01:19.530
OK, let's not waste a minute,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.815
and get right into it.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:26.066
It is October 1788.

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:29.229
At Windsor Castle, the King of

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:32.249
Great Britain is behaving strangely.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:35.190
He is talking without stopping.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:36.780
For hours.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.169
Sometimes through the night.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.310
His words make no sense.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.240
He speaks to people who

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.489
are not in the room.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.950
He wanders the corridors of the castle in

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.810
the early hours, agitated and incoherent.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:57.450
Then, one afternoon, he walks up to an

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:00.170
oak tree, takes hold of its branches,

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.010
and begins a conversation with it.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.209
He is apparently under the impression that

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:10.089
he is speaking with the King of Prussia.

00:02:11.579 --> 00:02:14.490
His courtiers do not know what to do.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.769
The King, King George III,

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.040
appears to be going mad.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.380
Now, if you know anything about

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.530
George III, it is probably

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:28.639
that he was the Mad King.

00:02:28.859 --> 00:02:30.240
The one who lost America.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.899
The one in the musical Hamilton,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.910
as a comic, petulant villain who

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:37.849
sings about how the colonists

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.650
will come crawling back to him.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.049
The one who ended his days raving

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:46.000
in a locked room at Windsor Castle.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.440
All of that might be true.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.560
But it is far from the whole story.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.229
Because for most of his sixty-year

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.839
reign, the longest of any male British

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.670
monarch in history, George III was a

00:03:02.679 --> 00:03:06.420
serious, devoted, rather ordinary man

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.940
who just so happened to be a king.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.480
A man who woke up before

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.430
dawn to check on his crops.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.000
Who wrote articles about

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.020
farming under a fake name.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.020
Who was unusually devoted to his wife.

00:03:21.199 --> 00:03:23.660
In today's episode, we are going to try

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.370
to set the "mad king" label aside for

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.109
a moment and look at the full picture.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:31.809
We'll start with who George

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:34.290
really was before his illness.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.456
We'll look at the turbulent kingdom

00:03:36.679 --> 00:03:39.160
he was theoretically the head of.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.299
We'll look at his illness, and

00:03:41.299 --> 00:03:43.060
the treatments that were almost

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:44.829
certainly making him worse.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.100
And we'll end with a question

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.530
that historians and doctors are

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.370
still arguing about today: what

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.190
was actually wrong with him?

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.469
So, George III was born in 1738.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:02.750
As the name suggests, he was the third

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:05.300
king called George; the other two

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:07.780
were his great-grandfather, George

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.959
I, and his grandfather, George II.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.746
And compared to the Georges

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.840
that came before him, he was

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.840
something of a fresh start.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:24.230
The two other Georges were both German,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.380
essentially, from the House of Hanover.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.340
Neither could really speak much English,

00:04:30.469 --> 00:04:32.670
and they had never bothered to learn.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:36.450
George III was different.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.419
He had been born in London, educated

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:43.740
in Britain, and was determined to

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.009
present himself not as a German

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.769
prince who happened to wear the

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.099
British crown, but as a British king.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.270
He was also unlike most other kings.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.370
While many European monarchs were

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.520
flamboyant, extravagant, obsessed with

00:05:01.559 --> 00:05:04.060
courts and ceremony and mistresses,

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.030
George was frugal and hardworking.

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:09.529
He got up early.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.239
He ate simply.

00:05:11.549 --> 00:05:12.520
He loved his wife.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.550
He had fifteen children with her, and by

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:18.020
most accounts, he loved his children too.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:21.510
He was the first British king to be

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.849
formally educated in the sciences.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.300
He had his own astronomical observatory.

00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:28.730
He collected maps.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.349
He collected clocks.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.289
He had an enormous personal

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.320
library that, after his death,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:36.760
was donated to what became the

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:40.579
British Library: 65,000 volumes.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:45.020
But his real passion was agriculture.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:49.040
Under the pen name Ralph Robinson,

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.940
he wrote articles about farming

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.520
methods for a publication called

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.120
the Annals of Agriculture.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.820
His readers had no idea they were reading

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:02.340
dispatches from the King of Great Britain.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.140
And it wasn't just theory, either.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.030
As the expression goes, he was more than

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:12.330
happy to get his hands dirty, to roll

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.880
up his sleeves and do the physical work

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.570
himself, rather than leave it to others.

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:20.950
He kept a working farm at Windsor.

00:06:21.359 --> 00:06:23.400
He went out before dawn

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.260
to check on his livestock.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.940
He would walk his own fields and sometimes

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.510
engage in conversation with labourers

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.370
who would assume he was just another

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.080
farmer, and would chat away happily

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:38.340
about crops and prices and the weather,

00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:41.179
never guessing who they were talking to.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.390
This was where the nickname

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.610
came from: Farmer George.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:50.509
It was meant, by some, as a mockery.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:52.800
The implication was that he

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.719
was too simple, too earthy, too

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.410
undignified to be a real king.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.049
But the British public,

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:03.549
apparently, rather liked it.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.570
A king who knew the price of wheat, who

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:09.030
went to bed at a reasonable hour, who had

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.469
not been caught in any scandalous affairs.

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:14.789
That was a king you could trust.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:17.230
He had other qualities too.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.080
He was devoted to his

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.326
wife, and a loyal husband.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.560
It was not a love marriage, as

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:27.349
the term goes: it was an arranged

00:07:27.349 --> 00:07:29.599
marriage, a political match.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:32.290
She was a German princess,

00:07:32.549 --> 00:07:34.439
he was an unmarried king.

00:07:35.429 --> 00:07:38.320
And they were both incredibly young.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.250
She arrived in England, met

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.229
George the following day and

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:45.650
married him the very same evening.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.080
She was seventeen.

00:07:48.379 --> 00:07:49.429
He was twenty-two.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.020
These things rarely went well.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:55.280
But this one did.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:58.200
By all accounts, George adored

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.460
her, and was faithful to

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.130
her for their 57-year union.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:06.139
He bought her a house, a place called

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.960
the Queen's House, which he had enlarged

00:08:09.159 --> 00:08:11.534
and redecorated specially for her.

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:13.510
You can visit it today,

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.410
and perhaps you have.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.300
You might know it by its

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:19.620
later name: Buckingham Palace.

00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:25.609
He was, in short, a dutiful, domestic

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:28.989
and decent man, someone who might have

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:32.330
lived a quieter and happier life had

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:34.900
he not been born in line to the throne.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.360
So, this is the man who

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.520
became known as the Mad King.

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:41.610
A farmer.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:42.670
A husband.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:43.739
A father.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:45.109
An avid reader.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:46.760
A man who loved clocks.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.130
Not the monster he is

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.720
sometimes portrayed as.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.720
Now, some monarchs are remembered in

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:57.010
the history books for their cunning and

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.030
intelligence, their ability to navigate

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:02.819
international politics, manage internal

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.420
unrest, and leave the country in a

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.600
stronger position than how they found it.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.410
George III might have been a perfectly

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.160
decent man in many respects, but

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.790
he was not one of these monarchs.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.590
And it just so happened that he was

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:23.240
landed with a series of incredibly

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:25.199
tricky problems to deal with.

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.390
Not immediately, I should say; when

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:32.620
he became king, in 1760, things were

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:33.980
looking pretty good for Britain.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:36.599
Shortly afterwards, the country

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:38.850
emerged victorious from the Seven

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:41.349
Years War, winning Britain vast

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:42.850
new territories across North

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:45.730
America, India, and the Caribbean.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:50.900
But this victory was a double-edged sword.

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:54.520
The country was drowning in debt,

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:57.639
and George was now responsible

00:09:57.679 --> 00:10:00.230
for a vast, difficult-to-govern

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.350
empire across the Atlantic.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:05.870
And then, within fifteen years,

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.610
this empire had started to fracture.

00:10:11.089 --> 00:10:12.970
The American colonies had been

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.230
growing increasingly restless.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:17.929
Taxation without representation.

00:10:18.249 --> 00:10:19.469
Trade restrictions.

00:10:19.743 --> 00:10:22.210
A growing sense that London was

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:24.950
governing people it did not understand

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:27.610
and did not particularly care about.

00:10:28.469 --> 00:10:30.810
George and his ministers did

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:32.470
not handle the situation well.

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:36.000
Reforms were proposed and then reversed.

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:37.540
Taxes were imposed,

00:10:38.118 --> 00:10:39.979
dropped, and then reimposed.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:42.970
The colonists were given every

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.669
reason to stop trusting the

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.880
intentions of the British Parliament.

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:51.289
And what came next, well,

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:52.850
it came pretty quickly.

00:10:53.860 --> 00:10:56.720
By 1775, it was war.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.130
By 1776, the colonies had

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:01.490
declared independence.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:04.500
By 1783, it was over.

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:06.630
Britain had lost.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:11.120
George III is often cast as the

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:13.559
villain of this story, at least

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.110
in the American popular memory.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:19.370
The reality is a little more complicated.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:22.620
George was a constitutional

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.760
monarch, not an absolute one.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.710
In other words, he could influence

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:31.040
policy, but it was the British Parliament

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:32.840
that ultimately called the shots,

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:34.969
that did the real decision-making.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.830
He was stubborn, certainly; he

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.990
resisted American independence

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:41.272
for longer than was sensible.

00:11:41.788 --> 00:11:45.050
And he was not a mere helpless spectator.

00:11:45.669 --> 00:11:49.390
But he was not single-handedly responsible

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.640
for the decisions that led to revolution,

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.459
and ultimately, American independence.

00:11:55.623 --> 00:11:57.513
What the loss of America did,

00:11:57.943 --> 00:12:00.303
undeniably, was wound him.

00:12:00.973 --> 00:12:02.503
He took it personally.

00:12:02.973 --> 00:12:05.003
He felt it as a failure.

00:12:05.623 --> 00:12:07.613
He even wrote a draft letter of

00:12:07.613 --> 00:12:10.623
abdication, where he would step down

00:12:10.653 --> 00:12:13.353
from the throne, before deciding to stay.

00:12:14.143 --> 00:12:16.623
And then the French Revolution began.

00:12:17.193 --> 00:12:18.243
Napoleon appeared.

00:12:18.713 --> 00:12:20.813
Britain found itself in almost

00:12:20.903 --> 00:12:23.093
constant war with France for

00:12:23.093 --> 00:12:25.483
the best part of twenty years.

00:12:26.233 --> 00:12:28.623
George III, Farmer George,

00:12:29.093 --> 00:12:30.683
he was not cut out for this.

00:12:31.103 --> 00:12:34.103
He was a quiet man who wanted to grow

00:12:34.103 --> 00:12:37.153
turnips and read about astronomy, and

00:12:37.353 --> 00:12:39.733
there he was, presiding over one of

00:12:39.733 --> 00:12:42.363
the most violent and consequential

00:12:42.363 --> 00:12:44.203
periods in modern history.

00:12:45.053 --> 00:12:47.393
The pressure was relentless.

00:12:48.373 --> 00:12:52.493
And in the autumn of 1788, when he

00:12:52.493 --> 00:12:56.063
was fifty years old, something broke.

00:12:57.273 --> 00:12:59.063
The first serious episode

00:12:59.393 --> 00:13:02.725
begins in October 1788.

00:13:03.063 --> 00:13:04.683
The symptoms arrive quickly.

00:13:05.163 --> 00:13:07.943
He becomes agitated and cannot sleep.

00:13:08.282 --> 00:13:10.532
He talks without stopping, for

00:13:10.632 --> 00:13:12.832
hours, sometimes through the night,

00:13:13.102 --> 00:13:15.242
the words tumbling over each other

00:13:15.502 --> 00:13:16.772
until they lose their meaning.

00:13:17.642 --> 00:13:19.472
He sees and speaks to

00:13:19.472 --> 00:13:21.492
people who are not there.

00:13:21.892 --> 00:13:23.202
He shakes the branches of an

00:13:23.202 --> 00:13:25.112
oak tree in Windsor Great Park,

00:13:25.312 --> 00:13:27.622
apparently believing himself to be in

00:13:27.622 --> 00:13:29.782
conversation with the King of Prussia.

00:13:31.142 --> 00:13:33.352
His household is terrified.

00:13:33.822 --> 00:13:36.482
Charlotte, his wife, is terrified.

00:13:37.262 --> 00:13:38.862
Doctors are called.

00:13:39.362 --> 00:13:41.212
And this is where the

00:13:41.212 --> 00:13:43.292
story takes a dark turn..

00:13:44.412 --> 00:13:46.776
The year is 1788.

00:13:47.291 --> 00:13:50.192
Nobody understands mental illness.

00:13:50.402 --> 00:13:53.542
There is no real vocabulary for it, no

00:13:53.942 --> 00:13:57.032
framework, and certainly no treatment

00:13:57.322 --> 00:13:58.762
that could do anything useful.

00:14:00.062 --> 00:14:02.712
What doctors had instead was a

00:14:02.742 --> 00:14:04.952
set of interventions built on

00:14:04.962 --> 00:14:06.752
theories about the body that were

00:14:06.762 --> 00:14:09.252
already centuries out of date.

00:14:10.672 --> 00:14:12.582
George's regular physicians

00:14:13.072 --> 00:14:14.642
have no idea what to do.

00:14:15.482 --> 00:14:17.762
They start by bleeding him.

00:14:18.312 --> 00:14:21.232
They give him laxatives and emetics,

00:14:21.422 --> 00:14:24.002
medicines designed to make him vomit.

00:14:24.992 --> 00:14:27.492
They apply what were called blistering

00:14:27.542 --> 00:14:30.842
agents to his legs: caustic chemicals that

00:14:30.842 --> 00:14:33.462
raise painful blisters on the skin, in

00:14:33.462 --> 00:14:36.452
the belief that whatever is wrong inside

00:14:36.772 --> 00:14:39.412
might somehow exit through the wound.

00:14:40.302 --> 00:14:42.022
This is mainstream

00:14:42.032 --> 00:14:44.666
medical practice in 1788.

00:14:45.812 --> 00:14:47.392
And you don't have to have a

00:14:47.402 --> 00:14:49.272
medical degree to guess that

00:14:49.272 --> 00:14:51.442
none of this does any good.

00:14:52.772 --> 00:14:54.272
After several weeks, a

00:14:54.302 --> 00:14:55.702
specialist is brought in.

00:14:56.602 --> 00:14:59.072
His name is Francis Willis.

00:15:00.022 --> 00:15:03.472
He is an unusual figure, a clergyman

00:15:03.472 --> 00:15:05.942
as well as a physician, but he has a

00:15:05.942 --> 00:15:08.942
reputation for treating the mentally ill.

00:15:09.502 --> 00:15:12.352
Parliament is desperate for the king to

00:15:12.352 --> 00:15:15.512
recover and is willing to try anything.

00:15:16.651 --> 00:15:19.661
Willis believes that mental illness is

00:15:19.701 --> 00:15:23.501
caused by what he calls over-excitement.

00:15:24.081 --> 00:15:26.441
The cure is control.

00:15:27.671 --> 00:15:30.911
His method is simple, and it is brutal.

00:15:31.821 --> 00:15:34.101
When the king falls into one of these

00:15:34.151 --> 00:15:36.911
episodes, when he shouts or attacks

00:15:36.921 --> 00:15:39.231
the attendants around him or becomes

00:15:39.261 --> 00:15:42.781
impossible to manage, Willis orders

00:15:42.971 --> 00:15:46.032
for him to be put into a straitjacket,

00:15:46.341 --> 00:15:47.971
those jackets where your arms are

00:15:47.991 --> 00:15:50.011
tied down securely around your body

00:15:50.311 --> 00:15:53.196
so you cannot move, the ones that are

00:15:53.196 --> 00:15:55.725
sometimes used for violent prisoners.

00:15:56.882 --> 00:15:59.801
He is left in it, thrashing and shouting,

00:16:00.141 --> 00:16:03.101
until he exhausts himself and goes quiet.

00:16:04.181 --> 00:16:07.441
When he is calm, he is rewarded.

00:16:08.101 --> 00:16:09.371
He is allowed to see

00:16:09.371 --> 00:16:10.991
Charlotte and the children.

00:16:11.521 --> 00:16:13.811
He is permitted small comforts.

00:16:14.321 --> 00:16:15.841
When he becomes difficult again,

00:16:16.541 --> 00:16:18.841
back into the jacket it is.

00:16:19.971 --> 00:16:22.711
Willis also chains the king to his

00:16:22.711 --> 00:16:24.951
chair when he deems it necessary.

00:16:25.571 --> 00:16:28.001
When the talking becomes uncontrollable,

00:16:28.211 --> 00:16:31.191
he has him gagged, a cloth put

00:16:31.231 --> 00:16:33.781
around his mouth so he cannot talk.

00:16:34.251 --> 00:16:37.291
The King of Great Britain, monarch of one

00:16:37.291 --> 00:16:39.851
of the most powerful states in the world,

00:16:40.241 --> 00:16:43.151
is being managed like a dangerous animal.

00:16:44.241 --> 00:16:46.081
The grim irony here is that

00:16:46.101 --> 00:16:48.681
Willis was considered a relatively

00:16:48.981 --> 00:16:50.431
enlightened practitioner.

00:16:51.281 --> 00:16:52.811
There were many institutions in

00:16:52.811 --> 00:16:55.131
England where the mentally ill were

00:16:55.131 --> 00:16:57.991
treated far, far worse: chained to

00:16:57.991 --> 00:17:00.981
walls, exhibited to the public, or

00:17:01.251 --> 00:17:04.191
simply left to rot in their own filth.

00:17:05.211 --> 00:17:07.461
Willis, at least, believed that

00:17:07.461 --> 00:17:09.931
patients could recover, and that

00:17:09.941 --> 00:17:12.111
this method was the most effective

00:17:12.111 --> 00:17:14.651
way of bringing about a recovery.

00:17:15.821 --> 00:17:17.651
Meanwhile, in Parliament,

00:17:17.821 --> 00:17:19.671
a crisis is unfolding.

00:17:20.601 --> 00:17:22.751
Parliament runs the day-to-day

00:17:22.751 --> 00:17:23.721
business of government.

00:17:24.051 --> 00:17:26.831
But many of the formal powers of the

00:17:26.831 --> 00:17:29.441
Crown, such as appointing ministers

00:17:29.441 --> 00:17:32.031
and giving royal assent to new laws,

00:17:32.481 --> 00:17:34.801
these require a functioning king.

00:17:35.411 --> 00:17:37.531
Someone needs to exercise

00:17:37.571 --> 00:17:39.331
those powers in his place.

00:17:40.721 --> 00:17:42.691
The question of who that should

00:17:42.701 --> 00:17:46.021
be, and on what terms, consumes

00:17:46.021 --> 00:17:48.251
the House of Commons for months.

00:17:49.231 --> 00:17:52.821
Then George recovers, and the crisis

00:17:52.821 --> 00:17:56.371
becomes, for the moment, moot, irrelevant.

00:17:57.251 --> 00:17:59.570
By February of 1789,

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:02.091
George begins to improve.

00:18:02.481 --> 00:18:04.511
By April, he is well enough

00:18:04.611 --> 00:18:06.501
to return to public life.

00:18:07.151 --> 00:18:09.201
The recovery is met with

00:18:09.221 --> 00:18:10.931
national celebration.

00:18:11.431 --> 00:18:12.311
Fireworks.

00:18:12.631 --> 00:18:14.901
Services of thanksgiving in churches

00:18:14.941 --> 00:18:17.791
across the country, and a grand

00:18:17.831 --> 00:18:19.991
ceremony at St Paul’s Cathedral.

00:18:20.691 --> 00:18:22.301
Farmer George, or rather

00:18:22.751 --> 00:18:24.721
King George, is back.

00:18:26.161 --> 00:18:28.491
Sadly, it would not last.

00:18:29.291 --> 00:18:31.915
Further episodes followed, in 1801

00:18:32.311 --> 00:18:35.651
and again in 1804, each one longer

00:18:35.871 --> 00:18:37.961
and more severe than the last.

00:18:38.751 --> 00:18:42.051
And then, in 1810, following the death of

00:18:42.051 --> 00:18:44.371
his youngest daughter, Princess Amelia,

00:18:44.851 --> 00:18:47.901
he began to decline for the final time.

00:18:48.871 --> 00:18:51.231
By 1811, it was clear

00:18:51.441 --> 00:18:52.941
that he would not recover.

00:18:53.531 --> 00:18:55.911
His eldest son, the future

00:18:55.961 --> 00:18:58.331
George IV, was appointed Prince

00:18:58.341 --> 00:19:00.471
Regent to govern in his place.

00:19:01.751 --> 00:19:03.421
George III was now

00:19:03.441 --> 00:19:05.401
confined to Windsor Castle.

00:19:06.691 --> 00:19:08.961
By this point, he was old,

00:19:09.251 --> 00:19:11.461
blind, and partially deaf.

00:19:12.301 --> 00:19:14.091
In his final years, his

00:19:14.091 --> 00:19:15.771
mind was gone entirely.

00:19:16.441 --> 00:19:18.631
He wandered the corridors at night,

00:19:18.891 --> 00:19:21.281
white-bearded and gaunt in his

00:19:21.281 --> 00:19:23.161
dressing gown, talking to people

00:19:23.571 --> 00:19:25.181
who had been dead for decades.

00:19:26.341 --> 00:19:29.141
He lived like this for nine more years,

00:19:29.401 --> 00:19:32.861
before his death in 1820, aged eighty-one.

00:19:34.281 --> 00:19:36.621
So, you might be asking yourself,

00:19:37.001 --> 00:19:39.051
what was actually wrong with him?

00:19:40.131 --> 00:19:42.331
This is the question that historians

00:19:42.331 --> 00:19:43.651
and doctors have been arguing

00:19:43.661 --> 00:19:45.831
about for more than a century.

00:19:46.891 --> 00:19:49.121
For most of the twentieth century,

00:19:49.611 --> 00:19:51.851
the accepted answer was something

00:19:51.851 --> 00:19:54.131
called porphyria, specifically

00:19:54.391 --> 00:19:56.401
variegate porphyria, which is

00:19:56.401 --> 00:19:58.491
a hereditary blood disorder.

00:19:59.571 --> 00:20:03.431
The theory was put forward in 1966 by

00:20:03.471 --> 00:20:06.171
two medical historians, who suggested

00:20:06.361 --> 00:20:08.501
that George's physical symptoms,

00:20:08.663 --> 00:20:11.519
such as discoloured urine and muscle

00:20:11.519 --> 00:20:14.449
weakness, these pointed to a metabolic

00:20:14.459 --> 00:20:17.089
disease rather than a psychiatric one.

00:20:17.919 --> 00:20:19.619
In other words, he wasn't a

00:20:19.619 --> 00:20:22.239
"madman"; this was a physical

00:20:22.239 --> 00:20:24.709
disease, not a mental illness.

00:20:26.029 --> 00:20:28.639
The theory gained significant support

00:20:28.879 --> 00:20:31.939
in the early 2000s, when scientists

00:20:32.169 --> 00:20:35.229
analysed samples of George's hair and

00:20:35.459 --> 00:20:38.639
found arsenic at levels roughly a hundred

00:20:38.639 --> 00:20:41.329
times higher than those in healthy people.

00:20:42.209 --> 00:20:44.469
The suggestion was that his doctors

00:20:44.669 --> 00:20:47.069
had been inadvertently poisoning

00:20:47.069 --> 00:20:49.459
him with arsenic-based medicines,

00:20:49.639 --> 00:20:51.229
which would have triggered and

00:20:51.379 --> 00:20:53.719
worsened any underlying condition.

00:20:55.129 --> 00:20:58.459
But from around 2010 onwards,

00:20:58.799 --> 00:21:01.229
this theory began to fall apart.

00:21:02.179 --> 00:21:05.189
A systematic review of George's actual

00:21:05.209 --> 00:21:07.919
medical records found that the two

00:21:07.929 --> 00:21:10.559
researchers had been highly selective.

00:21:10.959 --> 00:21:13.229
They had emphasised the physical

00:21:13.239 --> 00:21:16.579
symptoms and quietly downplayed, or

00:21:16.699 --> 00:21:19.319
simply omitted, the psychiatric ones.

00:21:20.259 --> 00:21:23.439
The current consensus is that George III

00:21:23.769 --> 00:21:26.479
is likely to have had bipolar disorder.

00:21:27.289 --> 00:21:29.759
Specifically, bipolar disorder

00:21:29.999 --> 00:21:32.469
type one, the kind characterised

00:21:32.489 --> 00:21:34.619
by full manic episodes.

00:21:35.129 --> 00:21:36.709
The racing thoughts.

00:21:36.729 --> 00:21:38.819
The inability to stop speaking.

00:21:38.829 --> 00:21:40.249
The delusions.

00:21:40.389 --> 00:21:41.779
The grandiosity.

00:21:41.819 --> 00:21:45.169
The weeks of extreme agitation followed

00:21:45.169 --> 00:21:47.599
by periods of relative normality.

00:21:48.179 --> 00:21:50.369
These are not the symptoms of

00:21:50.369 --> 00:21:52.899
a blood disease; they are the

00:21:52.899 --> 00:21:55.119
hallmarks of severe mania.

00:21:56.479 --> 00:21:58.119
As for the arsenic levels in

00:21:58.119 --> 00:22:00.369
his hair, these were accurate.

00:22:00.499 --> 00:22:02.609
The finding was not exaggerated.

00:22:03.379 --> 00:22:07.169
But this was most probably a consequence

00:22:07.339 --> 00:22:08.769
of the treatments he received,

00:22:09.019 --> 00:22:11.469
rather than a cause of his illness.

00:22:12.422 --> 00:22:16.022
Today, as you may know, bipolar disorder

00:22:16.272 --> 00:22:18.362
is a condition that can be managed

00:22:18.362 --> 00:22:21.502
with medication and therapy, and many

00:22:21.502 --> 00:22:23.662
people with bipolar disorder type

00:22:23.682 --> 00:22:26.872
one lead full and productive lives.

00:22:27.722 --> 00:22:30.471
But George had no luxury.

00:22:31.331 --> 00:22:33.632
Instead, he was strapped into a

00:22:33.642 --> 00:22:36.122
jacket and chained to a chair.

00:22:37.202 --> 00:22:39.772
And while the physicians might well have

00:22:39.812 --> 00:22:42.202
believed they were doing the right thing,

00:22:42.652 --> 00:22:45.032
indeed the only thing they could, they

00:22:45.032 --> 00:22:47.902
were almost certainly making it worse.

00:22:48.522 --> 00:22:51.252
The bleeding, the purging, the blistering,

00:22:51.682 --> 00:22:54.132
these would have weakened him physically.

00:22:54.862 --> 00:22:57.892
And the trauma of being restrained

00:22:57.922 --> 00:23:00.922
repeatedly, put into a straitjacket or

00:23:00.972 --> 00:23:04.032
tied to a chair, all this can hardly

00:23:04.032 --> 00:23:07.032
have calmed a mind already in crisis.

00:23:07.672 --> 00:23:09.902
It may well have intensified his

00:23:09.902 --> 00:23:13.052
fear, agitation and confusion, and

00:23:13.072 --> 00:23:15.512
triggered further manic episodes.

00:23:16.722 --> 00:23:20.232
George III died on the 29th of January

00:23:20.615 --> 00:23:23.952
1820 at Windsor Castle, aged eighty-one.

00:23:24.582 --> 00:23:26.732
He had been king for fifty-nine

00:23:26.732 --> 00:23:28.912
years and ninety-six days.

00:23:29.962 --> 00:23:32.762
In that time, Britain had lost

00:23:32.762 --> 00:23:35.932
America, defeated Napoleon, expanded

00:23:35.932 --> 00:23:38.542
its empire across six continents,

00:23:38.702 --> 00:23:41.442
and begun the Industrial Revolution.

00:23:42.042 --> 00:23:44.922
The world he left behind looked almost

00:23:44.942 --> 00:23:47.712
nothing like the world he had inherited.

00:23:48.922 --> 00:23:51.272
He had watched almost none of it.

00:23:51.311 --> 00:23:53.112
Not in the last decade, anyway.

00:23:53.852 --> 00:23:56.682
So, to wrap things up, the "mad

00:23:56.712 --> 00:23:58.622
king" remains one of history's

00:23:58.642 --> 00:24:00.542
most durable caricatures.

00:24:01.122 --> 00:24:04.002
In American memory, he is a tyrant.

00:24:04.322 --> 00:24:06.422
In British popular culture, a

00:24:06.422 --> 00:24:09.732
figure of pity or of gentle comedy.

00:24:10.522 --> 00:24:12.322
The reality was neither

00:24:12.362 --> 00:24:14.142
tyranny nor romance.

00:24:14.562 --> 00:24:18.012
It was a conscientious, earnest, somewhat

00:24:18.042 --> 00:24:21.072
ordinary man, placed on the throne during

00:24:21.112 --> 00:24:23.982
an extraordinary age, and then crushed

00:24:24.252 --> 00:24:26.922
by an illness nobody around him had the

00:24:26.922 --> 00:24:30.102
tools to understand, let alone to treat.

00:24:31.922 --> 00:24:33.652
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:24:33.652 --> 00:24:37.042
episode on George III, the Mad King.

00:24:37.402 --> 00:24:38.712
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:24:38.942 --> 00:24:40.532
and that you've learnt something new.

00:24:41.162 --> 00:24:42.902
As always, I would love to know

00:24:42.932 --> 00:24:44.232
what you thought of this episode.

00:24:44.452 --> 00:24:45.872
Let me know in the comments below,

00:24:46.062 --> 00:24:47.322
if you're listening to this somewhere

00:24:47.322 --> 00:24:49.042
where you can comment, and for the

00:24:49.042 --> 00:24:50.762
members among you, you can head right

00:24:50.792 --> 00:24:52.782
into our community forum, which is at

00:24:52.782 --> 00:24:55.522
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:24:55.522 --> 00:24:57.612
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:24:58.012 --> 00:25:00.922
And as a final reminder, if you enjoyed

00:25:00.922 --> 00:25:02.742
